s L41:     2. cite31†C.2 Exact Loss Decomposition and Recurrent Transport L42:       1. cite32†Exact target-depth loss decomposition. L43:       2. cite33†Optional smooth interpretation. L44:       3. cite34†Relation to local state-error transport. L45:       4. cite35†Connection to depth mismatch. L46:     3. cite36†C.3 A Contractive Ranking-Reversal Construction L47:       1. cite37†Shared contractive dynamics. L48:       2. cite38†Explicit trajectories and ranking reversal. L49:       3. cite39†Selection failure without window-estimation error. L50:       4. cite40†Scope. L51:     4. cite41†C.4 Proof of the Selection-Regret Bound L52:       1. cite42†Depth mismatch. L53:       2. cite43†Screening protocol and assumptions. L54:       3. cite44†Proof. L55:       4. cite45†Full-depth consequence. L56:       5. cite46†Assumptions and limits. L57:     5. cite47†C.5 Optimal-Candidate Preservation L58:       1. cite48†Interpretation and relation to the regret bound. L59:       2. cite49†Scope. L60:   12. cite50†D H20 Decoding Throughput L61:   13. cite51†E Evidence and Reproduction L62:   14. cite52†F Activation Distributions Across Transformer Blocks L63:   15. cite53†G Activation Distributions Across Recurrent Steps L64:     1. cite54†G.1 Activation Profiles L65:     2. cite55†G.2 Outlier-Channel Stability L66:   16. cite56†H Configuration Rankings Across Recurrent Depths L67: cite57†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L68: \nL69: arXiv:2610.05265v1 [cs.LG] 04 Oct 2026\nL70: # Loopy: Low-Bit Quantization Framework for Looped Language Models\nL71: \nL72: Zeyu LI Affiliation: The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology    Yipu ZHANG Affiliation: The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology    Jintao Chen Affiliation: The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology    Xin LI Affiliation: Duke Kunshan University    Wei ZHANG Affiliation: The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology\nL73: ###### Abstract\nL74: Looped language models provide a parameter-efficient way to scale iterative test-time computation by repeatedly executing a shared recurrent core. Post-training quantization (PTQ) can reduce the memory footprint and inference cost of looped language models, but errors introduced by a quantized shared core affect subsequent cores. Among PTQ methods, channel scaling and orthogonal rotations preserve the floating-point computation while producing representations with different quantization quality.\nL75: We find that quantization configuration candidate rankings can change with recurrent depth, motivating configuration selection at the target deployment depth. However, evaluating every candidate over the full calibration set at this depth is costly. We therefore propose Loopy, a PTQ framework that formulates shared-core quantization through a recurrent-depth-aware objective, selecting shared low-bit representations by their final prediction loss at the target deployment depth.\nL76: Channel scaling and orthogonal rotations parameterize the candidate representations. To approximately solve this selection problem efficiently, Loopy progressively allocates calibration windows to promising candidates while preserving complete target-depth execution, using only forward evaluations. Across eight settings, Loopy achieves the state-of-the-art results among different baselines. On Ouro-1.4B under W4A4, Loopy reduces LAMBADA perplexity by 36.5% relative to SpinQuant.\nL77: Our code is available at cite58†https://github.com/Shameless0817/Loopy-review.git†github.com .\nL78: ## 1 Introduction\nL79: Looped language models increase effective depth by repeatedly executing a shared recurrent core composed of transformer blocks, enabling iterative computation without storing a separate set of weights for every recurrent step as shown in Figure cite59†1 (cite60†Dehghani et al., 2019 ; cite61†Zhu et al., 2025 ; cite62†Saunshi et al., 2025 ; cite63†Geiping et al., 2025 ).\nL80: Post-training quantization (PTQ) provides a complementary approach to efficient deployment by reducing the precision of weights and activations, thereby lowering memory requirements and enabling faster inference with low-bit kernels (cite64†Xiao et al., 2023 ; cite65†Ashkboos et al., 2024 ; cite66†Liu et al., 2025 ). Applying PTQ to looped language models therefore offers an effective solution to low-bit computation while preserving prediction quality.\nL81: cite67†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Looped language model.\nL82: \nL83: Channel scaling and orthogonal rotations mitigate quantization outliers while preserving the floating-point computation (cite64†Xiao et al., 2023 ; cite68†Lin et al., 2024 ; cite65†Ashkboos et al., 2024 ; cite66†Liu et al., 2025 ). However, these transformations do not uniquely determine a good quantization configuration (cite66†Liu et al., 2025 ).\nL84: To preserve parameter sharing, we use the same quantized weights and transformations across recurrent steps (Section cite11†3.1 ). As hidden states evolve across recurrent steps (Figure cite69†14 ), quantization errors propagate through subsequent computation. Consequently, smaller local reconstruction error need not imply lower final prediction loss (Appendix cite31†C.2 ). LoopQ also considers recurrent dynamics through loop-aware quantization (cite70†Fang et al., 2026 ).\nL85: These observations motivate target-depth evaluation, supported by our calibration-depth ablations (Figure cite71†7 ). However, evaluating every candidate on all calibration windows at this depth is costly.\nL86: Motivated by these observations, we introduce Loopy, a recurrent-depth-aware PTQ framework for looped language models, as shown in Figure cite72†2 . The key idea is to formulate shared-core quantization as representation selection under a target-depth prediction objective: a shared quantized representation is evaluated by its final prediction loss after complete execution at the intended recurrent depth. Channel scaling and orthogonal rotations define the candidate representation space.\nL87: Loopy approximately solves the resulting selection problem through progressive full-depth evaluation, keeping the recurrent depth fixed while allocating additional calibration windows to surviving candidates. This procedure uses only forward evaluations, and the selected quantized weights and transformations remain shared across recurrent steps.\nL88: cite73†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Loopy method overview. Top: channel scaling and rotation define quantized representations shared across recurrent steps. Bottom: progressive selection allocates more calibration windows to surviving candidates while preserving the target depth $T$.\nL89: We evaluate Loopy on three representative looped language models: the Ouro series (cite61†Zhu et al., 2025 ), Huginn (cite63†Geiping et al., 2025 ), and Recurrent-Llama (cite74†McLeish et al., 2025 ), under both W4A8 and W4A4 quantization. Our main contributions are summarized as follows.\nL90: \nL91:   * •\nL92: We systematically study low-bit quantization in looped language models. We uncover a loop-specific quantization phenomenon: a quantization configuration shared across recurrent steps can exhibit depth-dependent quality because of the recurrent dependency of looped models. This serves as the motivation for our method Loopy.\nL93: \nL94:   * •\nL95: We introduce Loopy, a post-training quantization framework centered on a recurrent-depth-aware objective for selecting shared low-bit representations. Loopy parameterizes these representations through channel scaling and orthogonal rotations, and approximately solves the selection problem through progressive full-depth evaluation. This procedure preserves the target recurrent depth while reducing calibration work by allocating additional windows only to surviving candidates.\nL96: \nL97:   * •\nL98: We evaluate Loopy across multiple looped language model families under W4A4 and W4A8 quantization. On Ouro-1.4B under W4A4, Loopy reduces LAMBADA perplexity by 36.5% relative to the adapted SpinQuant baseline. We further evaluate Loopy with low-bit KV quantization and show that Q/K Hadamard transforms substantially mitigate the degradation from 4-bit KV quantization.\nL99: ## 2 Motivation\nL100: \nL101: In a looped language model, a shared recurrent core repeatedly updates the hidden state according to\nL102: \nL103:  | $$h_{t+1}=F(h_{t};W),\\qquad t=0,\\ldots,T-1,$$  |\nL104: where $W$ denotes the shared weights and $T$ is the recurrent depth. After quantization, the corresponding computation becomes $\\hat{h}_{t+1}=\\hat{F}_{c}(\\hat{h}_{t})$, where $c$ specifies the quantized weights and transformation settings of the core’s linear operators. The configuration $c$ is shared across recurrent steps, while hidden states and dynamic activation scales may vary. Appendix cite53†G illustrates activation changes across recurrent calls.\nL105: ### 2.1 Rotation Mitigates Activation Outliers\nL106: \nL107: We examine how rotation affects activation quantization on Ouro-1.4B. Figure cite75†3 (a) shows reduced activation peaks in the displayed block’s attention and FFN inputs. We measure A4 reconstruction error using normalized mean-squared error (NMSE), $\\|Q(a)-a\\|_{2}^{2}/\\|a\\|_{2}^{2}$, where $Q$ denotes activation quantization. Figure cite75†3 (b) shows lower median NMSE after rotation for both inputs, motivating the shared rotation transforms in Section cite11†3.1 .\nL108: cite76†Image: Refer to caption L109: \nL110: (a) Activation magnitudes.\nL111: \nL112: (b) A4 NMSE.\nL113: \nL114: Figure 3: Rotation improves activation-side quantization on Ouro-1.4B. (a) Attention and feed-forward network (FFN) inputs of physical block 22 at recurrent step 4, before and after rotation. Appendix cite52†F provides all 24 blocks. (b) Activation normalized mean-squared error (NMSE) distributions without and with rotation, on a logarithmic scale.\nL115: ### 2.2 Configuration Quality Depends on Recurrent Depth\nL116: \nL117: To examine whether configuration quality depends on recurrent depth, we evaluate 20 rotation–scaling configurations on Ouro-1.4B W4A4 at depths $T=1,\\ldots,4$. Figure cite77†4 shows changes in their relative WinoGrande performance across depths, with the corresponding rankings provided in Appendix cite56†H .\nL118: cite78†Image: Refer to caption Figure 4: Accuracy across recurrent depths. WinoGrande accuracy (%) for 20 configurations of Ouro-1.4B W4A4. Columns identify scaling power $p$ and rotation seed $s$ and are ordered by decreasing accuracy at $T=4$. Rows show recurrent depth. A shared color scale preserves absolute differences across depths. Appendix cite56†H shows the corresponding within-depth rankings.\nL119: Together with the recurrent error analysis in Appendix cite29†C , these observations motivate selecting shared representations by their target-depth prediction loss, formalized in Section cite10†3 .\nL120: ## 3 Method\nL121: \nL122: Loopy formulates shared-core quantization as recurrent-depth-aware representation selection. Each candidate specifies quantized weights and transformations that remain shared across recurrent steps, while activation distributions evolve with the hidden state. We evaluate each candidate through complete execution at the intended deployment depth $T$. Given a candidate space $\\mathcal{C}$ and calibration windows $\\mathcal{D}$, we define the recurrent-depth-aware quantization objective as\nL123:  | $$c_{T}^{\\star}\\in\\arg\\min_{c\\in\\mathcal{C}}\\mathcal{L}_{T}(c;\\mathcal{D}),\\qquad\\mathcal{L}_{T}(c;\\mathcal{D})=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{D}|}\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal{D}}L_{T}(c,x),$$  |  | (1)\nL124: Here, $L_{T}(c,x)$ denotes the mean next-token negative log-likelihood (NLL) on window $x$ after $T$ recurrent steps. This objective evaluates the quality of a shared quantized representation through its complete target-depth recurrent computation. Channel scaling and orthogonal rotations parameterize the candidate space, while progressive full-depth selection provides an efficient solution.\nL125: ### 3.1 Shared Quantization Representations\nL126: \nL127: Each candidate $c=(s,p,b)$ specifies a rotation seed, a channel-scaling power, and a Hadamard block-size setting. The same configuration determines operator-specific transforms $D_{\\ell,p}$ and $R_{\\ell,s,b}$, which remain fixed across recurrent calls. For each linear operator $\\ell$, the paired transforms satisfy\nL128: \nL129:  | $$xW_{\\ell}^{\\top}=\\left(xD_{\\ell,p}^{-1}R_{\\ell,s,b}\\right)\\left(W_{\\ell}D_{\\ell,p}R_{\\ell,s,b}\\right)^{\\top}.$$  |  | (2)\nL130: In exact arithmetic, these transforms preserve the recurrent hidden-state trajectory (Appendix cite30†C.1 ). Quantization generally breaks this equivalence, motivating selection by final prediction quality.\nL131: \nL132: For each recurrent linear operator $\\ell$, we obtain a positive channel-scaling vector $d_{\\ell}$ from calibration and construct\nL133:  | $$D_{\\ell,p}=\\operatorname{diag}\\!\\left(\\frac{d_{\\ell}^{\\,p}}{\\sqrt{\\max(d_{\\ell}^{\\,p})\\min(d_{\\ell}^{\\,p})}}\\right),$$  |  | (3)\nL134:  | $$R_{\\ell,s,b}=S_{\\ell,s}^{\\mathrm{pre}}\\operatorname{blkdiag}(H_{b},\\ldots,H_{b})S_{\\ell,s}^{\\mathrm{post}},$$  |  | (4)\nL135: where powers are applied elementwise, $H_{b}$ is a normalized Hadamard matrix of size $b$, and $S_{\\ell,s}^{\\mathrm{pre}}$ and $S_{\\ell,s}^{\\mathrm{post}}$ are diagonal sign matrices generated deterministically from the seed and operator identity. We use randomized Hadamard rotations (cite65†Ashkboos et al., 2024 ), restricting $b$ to supported powers of two that divide the operator’s input dimension. Search varies $(s,p,b)$ while keeping the calibrated scaling vectors fixed.\nL136: For each candidate, we quantize the transformed weights and dynamically quantize the transformed inputs, yielding\nL137: \nL138:  | $$\\widehat{f}_{\\ell,c}(x)=Q_{a}\\!\\left(xD_{\\ell,p}^{-1}R_{\\ell,s,b}\\right)Q_{w}\\!\\left(W_{\\ell}D_{\\ell,p}R_{\\ell,s,b}\\right)^{\\top},$$  |  | (5)\nL139: Here, $Q_{w}$ and $Q_{a}$ denote weight and activation quantization. Weight quantization is fixed for each candidate, whereas activation scales are computed dynamically per token. The transforms act locally at linear operators, leaving residual-stream coordinates unchanged and requiring no step-specific weight copies. Figure cite79†5 illustrates these paired transforms in attention and FFN operators.\nL140: Figure 5: Operator-wise quantization transforms. Channel scaling and rotation in attention and FFN, with weight-side transforms merged before quantization and input transforms applied online.\nL141: ### 3.2 Progressive Full-Depth Selection\nL142: \nL143: Using fewer calibration windows estimates the same target-depth objective, whereas reducing recurrent depth changes the computation being evaluated. Loopy therefore progressively allocates windows to surviving candidates while preserving complete target-depth execution (Figure cite72†2 ).\nL144: Following successive halving (cite80†Jamieson & Talwalkar, 2016 ), we construct nested calibration subsets $\\mathcal{D}_{1}\\subset\\cdots\\subset\\mathcal{D}_{R}=\\mathcal{D}$ and initialize $\\mathcal{C}_{1}=\\mathcal{C}$. At stage $r$, all active candidates are evaluated on the same windows through complete $T$-step execution:\nL145: \nL146:  | $$\\widehat{\\mathcal{L}}_{r}(c)=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{D}_{r}|}\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal{D}_{r}}L_{T}(c,x),\\qquad c\\in\\mathcal{C}_{r}.$$  |  | (6)\nL147: We reuse previously computed losses and evaluate only newly added windows at each stage.\nL148: \nL149: After each intermediate stage, we retain the $K_{r+1}$ candidates with the lowest estimated loss,\nL150: \nL151:  | $$\\mathcal{C}_{r+1}=\\operatorname{Lowest}_{K_{r+1}}\\!\\left(\\mathcal{C}_{r};\\widehat{\\mathcal{L}}_{r}\\right),\\qquad r=1,\\ldots,R-1,$$  |  | (7)\nL152: where the sample budgets and retained candidate counts are specified before search. All candidates within a stage are compared on the same calibration windows, and ties are resolved using a fixed candidate order. As the active set shrinks, additional windows refine the comparison among the remaining configurations. At the final stage, every surviving candidate is evaluated on the full calibration set, and we select\nL153: \nL154:  | $$\\hat{c}_{T}=\\arg\\min_{c\\in\\mathcal{C}_{R}}\\mathcal{L}_{T}(c;\\mathcal{D}).$$  |  | (8)\nL155: Early elimination may discard the full-search optimum. A sufficient condition for preserving it is that, at each screening stage, the full-set loss gap between the optimum and candidates beyond the retention cutoff exceeds twice the maximum estimation error (Appendix cite47†C.5 ). This condition motivates allocating more windows to refine candidate comparisons while retaining enough candidates to accommodate uncertainty.\nL156: We assess the resulting approximation empirically through selection regret and prediction quality on separate evaluation data.\nL157: Let $n_{r}=|\\mathcal{D}_{r}|$, with $n_{0}=0$. By reusing previously computed losses, Loopy requires\nL158: \nL159:  | $$N_{\\mathrm{eval}}=\\sum_{r=1}^{R}|\\mathcal{C}_{r}|(n_{r}-n_{r-1})$$  |  | (9)\nL160: candidate–window evaluations, compared with $|\\mathcal{C}|\\,|\\mathcal{D}|$ for exhaustive search. This count measures evaluation work, and wall-clock cost also includes candidate construction and scheduling. At deployment, the selected transformed weights are precomputed and quantized, while input transforms and dynamic activation quantization are applied during execution. Algorithm cite81†1 summarizes the selection procedure.\nL161: In Algorithm cite81†1 , BuildOrRestore constructs or restores candidates from fixed calibration bases, and EvalFullDepth sums window-level mean NLLs after $T$ recurrent steps.\nL162: Algorithm 1 Loopy Progressive Full-Depth Selection\nL163: \nL164: 1: $\\mathcal{C}$, $T$, nested subsets $\\{\\mathcal{D}_{r}\\}_{r=1}^{R}$, retained counts $\\{K_{r}\\}_{r=2}^{R}$\nL165: \nL166: 2: $\\mathcal{C}_{1}\\leftarrow\\mathcal{C}$, $\\mathcal{D}_{0}\\leftarrow\\varnothing$, $S(c)\\leftarrow 0$ for all $c\\in\\mathcal{C}$\nL167: \nL168: 3: for $r=1,\\ldots,R$ do\nL169: \nL170: 4:   for $c\\in\\mathcal{C}_{r}$ do\nL171: \nL172: 5:    $\\widehat{F}_{c}\\leftarrow\\textsc{BuildOrRestore}(c)$\nL173: 6:    $S(c)\\leftarrow S(c)+\\textsc{EvalFullDepth}(\\widehat{F}_{c},T,\\mathcal{D}_{r}\\setminus\\mathcal{D}_{r-1})$\nL174: \nL175: 7:    $\\widehat{\\mathcal{L}}_{r}(c)\\leftarrow S(c)/|\\mathcal{D}_{r}|$\nL176: \nL177: 8:   end for\nL178: \nL179: 9:   if $r<R$ then\nL180: \nL181: 10:    $\\mathcal{C}_{r+1}\\leftarrow\\textsc{KeepLowest}(\\mathcal{C}_{r},\\widehat{\\mathcal{L}}_{r},K_{r+1})$\nL182: \nL183: 11:   end if\nL184: \nL185: 12: end for\nL186: \nL187: 13: $\\hat{c}_{T}\\leftarrow\\textsc{Best}(\\mathcal{C}_{R},\\widehat{\\mathcal{L}}_{R})$\nL188: \nL189: 14: return $\\textsc{BuildOrRestore}(\\hat{c}_{T})$\nL190: ### 3.3 Target-Depth Selection Analysis\nL191: \nL192: Recurrent error propagation can reverse candidate rankings across depths, even under shared dynamics (Appendix cite36†C.3 ). We therefore distinguish depth mismatch from finite-window estimation error.\nL193: \nL194: Fix the candidate pool $\\mathcal{C}$ and calibration set $\\mathcal{D}$, and abbreviate $\\mathcal{L}_{t}(c;\\mathcal{D})$ as $\\mathcal{L}_{t}(c)$. Define the pairwise depth mismatch\nL195:  | $$\\beta_{\\tau,T}=\\max_{c,c^{\\prime}\\in\\mathcal{C}}\\left|[\\mathcal{L}_{\\tau}(c)-\\mathcal{L}_{\\tau}(c^{\\prime})]-[\\mathcal{L}_{T}(c)-\\mathcal{L}_{T}(c^{\\prime})]\\right|.$$  |  | (10)\nL196: \nL197: This measures changes in candidate comparisons rather than candidate-independent loss shifts.\nL198: Selection-regret bound. Consider nested top-$K$ screening of fixed candidates, with stage $r<R$ evaluated at depth $\\tau_{r}$. Suppose $\\max_{c\\in\\mathcal{C}_{r}}|\\widehat{\\mathcal{L}}_{r}(c)-\\mathcal{L}_{\\tau_{r}}(c)|\\leq\\varepsilon_{r}$, and final selection minimizes $\\mathcal{L}_{T}$ over the survivors using all of $\\mathcal{D}$. Then\nL199:  | $\\displaystyle\\operatorname{Reg}_{T}(\\hat{c}_{T})$  | $\\displaystyle:=\\mathcal{L}_{T}(\\hat{c}_{T})-\\min_{c\\in\\mathcal{C}}\\mathcal{L}_{T}(c)$  |  | (11)\nL200:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\leq\\max_{r<R}\\bigl(\\beta_{\\tau_{r},T}+2\\varepsilon_{r}\\bigr).$  |\nL201: \nL202: The bound does not require a unique optimum or its survival. If the last global optimum is discarded, every candidate retained at that stage satisfies the bound, as do all subsequent survivors.\nL203: \nL204: For Loopy, $\\tau_{r}=T$ and $\\beta_{T,T}=0$, giving\nL205:  | $$\\operatorname{Reg}_{T}(\\hat{c}_{T})\\leq 2\\max_{r<R}\\varepsilon_{r}.$$  |  | (12)\nL206: \nL207: Full-depth screening removes depth mismatch, leaving window-estimation error. This conditional bound applies to the fixed calibration set. Our schedule does not certify $\\varepsilon_{r}$, and the bound guarantees neither held-out generalization nor superiority at equal compute. Appendix cite41†C.4 proves the regret bound, and Appendix cite47†C.5 gives a sufficient condition for exact optimum recovery.\nL208: ## 4 Experiments\nL209: ### 4.1 Setup\nL210: Models and evaluation. We evaluate Ouro-1.4B and Ouro-2.6B at recurrent depth $T=4$ (cite61†Zhu et al., 2025 ), Huginn-0125 at $T=32$ (cite63†Geiping et al., 2025 ), and Recurrent-Llama-3.2-1B at $T=16$ (cite74†McLeish et al., 2025 ).\nL211: We report perplexity on WikiText-2 (cite82†Merity et al., 2016 ) and LAMBADA (cite83†Paperno et al., 2016 ), and zero-shot accuracy on HellaSwag (cite84†Zellers et al., 2019 ), WinoGrande (cite85†Sakaguchi et al., 2020 ), LAMBADA, ARC-Challenge (cite86†Clark et al., 2018 ), and MMLU (cite87†Hendrycks et al., 2021 ). Avg. denotes the unweighted mean of the five task accuracies.\nL212: Quantization and baselines. We evaluate W4A4 and W4A8 against recurrent adaptations of AWQ, SmoothQuant, QuaRot, SpinQuant, and FlatQuant (cite68†Lin et al., 2024 ; cite64†Xiao et al., 2023 ; cite65†Ashkboos et al., 2024 ; cite66†Liu et al., 2025 ; cite88†Sun et al., 2024 ). Loopy and all evaluated baselines uses symmetric group-32 quantization and symmetric per-token activation quantization. Baseline-specific quantization settings and calibration procedures are detailed in Appendix cite26†B .\nL213: LoopQ comparison uses the same Loopy results as the main table and published LoopQ results (cite70†Fang et al., 2026 ).\nL214: Calibration and selection. Loopy selects shared rotation and scaling configurations using progressive full-depth evaluation (Section cite10†3 ). Calibration bases remain fixed across candidates; nested window subsets and retained candidate counts are specified before search. All Loopy results in Table cite89†1 and the full-results appendix are obtained using configurations selected by the progressive full-depth procedure described in Section cite10†3 .\nL215: Implementation. Main accuracy results use quantization and dequantization in BF16 with KV caching disabled. We implement packed low-bit inference in Triton (cite90†Tillet et al., 2019 ), with hardware benchmarks described in Section cite19†4.5 .\nL216: ### 4.2 Main Results\nL217: \nL218: Table 1: W4A8 and W4A4 results on looped language models. WT2 and LMB denote WikiText-2 and LAMBADA perplexity ($\\downarrow$); Avg. is mean task accuracy (%, $\\uparrow$). Detailed accuracy results are shown in Tables cite91†5 and cite92†6 . Bold indicates the best low-bit result per model and precision.\nL219:  | Ouro-2.6B  | Ouro-1.4B  | Huginn-0125  | Recurrent-Llama-3.2-1B\nL220: Method  | WT2  | LMB  | Avg.  | W